A||N||5||johnmac||tranaxle & rear suspension HELP||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||05-30-2001||10:36 PM||||1) I am trying to remove the tranaxle on my 86 GT Automatic --- how do I do it?<P>I have tried the screwdriver and lug iron method but the tranaxle is not slipping out.<P>Any suggestions?<P>2) All the rubber boots on the ball joints and tierods need replacing. How do I remove and replace the balljoint to insert a new rubber bootie. Any suggestions?<P>3) How do I remove the tierod end. The pitchfolk method does not work - it is frozen solid. Any suggestions?<P>Any suggestions would be appreciated.<P>Thanks,<P>John MacHaffie<BR> <P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||05-30-2001||11:26 PM||||Hey John!<BR>On using the pickle fork, get a BFH...<BR>I used an old style wrecking bar to pop out the axles...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||thomas_l||05-31-2001||09:16 AM||||I am guessing you mean you are having trouble getting the halfshafts to pop out. fierowrecker is right - the automatics are tough and you need leverage with a long pry bar. Just be careful about prying against any soft (aluminum) parts and use wood blocks to spread the pressure. If your pickle fork won't separate a joint you aren't hitting it hard enough with a large enough hammer. Or there is enough slop in the two parts to absorb the impact (ie bad control arm bushings, etc). I use a 55oz steel-faced dead blow hammer. Alternately, you can get those things to release without using a pickle fork by smacking the steering knuckle right where the pin goes thru (both tie rods and ball joints). If you've never done that or had someone show you how, you probably ought to stick to the pickle fork so you don't break something.||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||05-31-2001||10:31 AM||||John, ditto for advice on the halfshafts.  They have to 'pop' out so definitely use wood to protect the casing.<P>  On the tie rod ends I have given up on pickle forks and use a gear puller when possible.  If you have room (I have used it on the rear knuckles) it will pop out very easily. Turn the nut over a spin it down even with the end of the bolt surface, put the puller on and crank. GL||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||05-31-2001||10:51 AM||||I use a tie-rod puller that I rented from AutoZone.  You get the fee back when you return the tool.  I have since bought one at HarborFreight for $5.99 US.||||reg||1
Z||000005||johnmac||06-01-2001||07:42 AM||||WE GOT IT!!!<P>Thanks for the good advice. Got the axle out with junkyard bar and removed the tie rod end with a puller from Autozone.<P>You Fiero guys are great.<P>Thanks again.<P>John MacHaffie<P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
